# Target C-II — `g=0.0050` raises the joint orbit-capacity / phase-energy floor

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Repository base:** current `main` through the joint orbit-capacity/phase-energy theorem, plus the hostile-audited iterative-pruning atomic gap `g=0.0050`  
**Status:** proof-only low-`t` Type-II strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** same fully force-visible no-container/full-core Type-II scope as `research/target_c_joint_orbit_capacity_phase_energy_2026-09-15.md`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`  

The merged joint orbit-capacity/phase-energy theorem is symbolic in the atomic all-train floor

\[
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+g,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

Current `main` instantiated that theorem only with the older conservative value
`g=0.00042`, obtaining the safe numerical floor `t >= 1.82434-o(1)` for

\[
t={q-k\over D}.
\]

The independently hostile-audited iterative-pruning minimax theorem imported on this branch proves the stronger matched all-train/rhombic value

\[
\boxed{g=0.0050.}
\]

No geometric step of the joint orbit theorem changes. Re-running its one-dimensional endpoint with this stronger input yields

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}\ge1.82515.
}
\]

The scalar zero is approximately

\[
1.82516142463113334\ldots,
\]

but only the safely rounded `1.82515` is claimed.

C-II remains open.

---

## 1. Stronger atomic input

Put

\[
s_2:=\sqrt{\Delta_2}.
\]

The imported iterative-pruning theorem and its hostile audit prove, at the same proof-only train-measure scope,

\[
\boxed{
{T-q/2\over D}\ge C_0-o(1),
\qquad
C_0:=2s_2+0.0050.
}
\tag{GJ.1}
\]

They also prove the matched rhombic-only bounds

\[
{T_R\over D}\ge C_0-o(1),
\qquad
{\sigma_R\over D}\ge0.0100-o(1),
\tag{GJ.2}
\]

though only (GJ.1) is needed for the scalar improvement below.

The exact outward certificate

`research/verify_target_c_iterative_minimax_atomic_gap_0050.py`

proves the `0.0050` gap with more than `0.0015` margin over the geometric cap in its worst rational interval.

---

## 2. Retain the merged joint-orbit inequalities

On a convergent Type-II subsequence write

\[
t={q-k\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D}.
\]

As in the merged joint theorem, put

\[
U={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-\frac\kappa2.
\]

The Section-171 envelope and (GJ.1) give

\[
C_0\le C={T-q/2\over D}\le U,
\]

hence

\[
\boxed{
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}
\le R_{50}(t):={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0.
}
\tag{GJ.3}
\]

Canonical Sections 142/168 and the exposed-hull train endpoint inequality give one dominant exact `pi/3` orbit whose normalized outer terminal-occurrence mass obeys

\[
\boxed{
K\ge K_*(t,\delta,\kappa)
:=\pi+\delta-t-\kappa-2U+{8\Delta_2\over U}.
}
\tag{GJ.4}
\]

The merged joint route relaxation is exact for the two local constraints

\[
d_i\ge n_i g_i,
\qquad
d_i\ge n_i-s_i,
\]

and yields

\[
\boxed{
\delta\ge\Phi(K_*),
}
\tag{GJ.5}
\]

where

\[
\boxed{
\Phi(z)=z-6\arcsin(\tanh(z/6)).
}
\tag{GJ.6}
\]

Nothing in the derivation of (GJ.4)--(GJ.6) uses the old numerical value `0.00042`; it uses only the lower bound `C>=C_0`.

---

## 3. The two-parameter endpoint remains the same

For fixed `t,delta`,

\[
{\partial K_*\over\partial\kappa}
={4\Delta_2\over U^2}>0,
\]

so `Phi(K_*)-delta` is minimized at

\[
\kappa=0.
\]

For fixed `t,kappa`,

\[
{\partial K_*\over\partial\delta}
=5+{16\Delta_2\over U^2}.
\]

The stronger atomic floor gives `U>=C_0`, and the exact certificate checks

\[
5+{16\Delta_2\over C_0^2}
=8.9790809080\ldots<9.
\tag{GJ.7}
\]

For the relevant orbit masses below `1.33`,

\[
\Phi'(z)=1-\operatorname{sech}(z/6)<0.025.
\]

Therefore

\[
{\partial\over\partial\delta}
[\Phi(K_*)-\delta]
<9(0.025)-1<0.
\]

Thus the easiest feasible point remains the maximal-detour endpoint

\[
\boxed{
\kappa=0,
\qquad
\delta={R_{50}(t)\over2},
\qquad
U=C_0.
}
\tag{GJ.8}
\]

At this endpoint define

\[
K_{50}(t)
:=
\pi+{R_{50}(t)\over2}-t-2C_0+{8\Delta_2\over C_0}
\tag{GJ.9}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
H_{50}(t)
:=\Phi(K_{50}(t))-{R_{50}(t)\over2}.
}
\tag{GJ.10}
\]

A survivor requires

\[
H_{50}(t)\le0.
\tag{GJ.11}
\]

As in the merged theorem,

\[
K_{50}'(t)={5\over4}
\]

and hence

\[
H_{50}'(t)
={5\over4}\Phi'(K_{50}(t))-{9\over4}<0
\]

throughout the interval under consideration.

---

## 4. Certified endpoint

The branch verifier

`research/verify_target_c_joint_orbit_capacity_energy_g0050.py`

uses 100-digit `Decimal` arithmetic and a monotone bisection evaluation of the exact dual optimizer. It checks

\[
H_{50}(1.82515)
=2.5373341233\times10^{-5}>0,
\tag{GJ.12}
\]

\[
H_{50}(1.82516)
=3.1640100\times10^{-6}>0,
\]

and

\[
H_{50}(1.82517)
=-1.9045315\times10^{-5}<0.
\]

It further brackets the scalar zero by

\[
1.82516142463113334
<t_*<
1.82516142463113335.
\tag{GJ.13}
\]

The stronger `g=0.0050` Section-171 floor

\[
t_{50,\mathrm{base}}
={4\over9}(\pi+s_2)+0.0023374
=1.8218509379\ldots
\]

has joint-orbit contradiction margin greater than `0.00735`, so the whole interval from that prior floor through `1.82515` is excluded.

### Theorem GJ.1 — strengthened joint orbit floor

Every convergent full-core Type-II survivor subsequence in the proof-only scope satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{q-k\over D}
\ge1.82515.
}
\tag{GJ.14}
\]

This numerically supersedes the `1.82434` conclusion of the merged joint orbit-capacity/phase-energy theorem.

---

## 5. What changes at the frontier

The improvement is small in absolute decimal size but not merely cosmetic. The proof now simultaneously carries

\[
{T-q/2\over D}\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0050-o(1),
\]

\[
{T_R\over D}\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0050-o(1),
\qquad
{\sigma_R\over D}\ge0.0100-o(1),
\]

and

\[
{q-k\over D}\ge1.82515-o(1).
\]

The joint orbit relaxation has already optimized the two local outer-route costs exactly. Further progress should not come from re-combining those same scalar inequalities. The remaining useful interfaces are structural:

1. route the dominant-orbit terminal mass into bounded exceptional faces versus actual outer-to-outer trains;
2. combine the resulting outer-to-outer population with the response-free two-sided diameter bracing theorem;
3. use the matched rhombic slack `sigma_R/D>=0.0100` in the exceptional-face closure-rank ledger; or
4. produce a simultaneous source-preserving multi-train shear in the near-equality orbit configuration.

No exact unit equilateral triangle or strict same-cardinality sub-`D` competitor is produced here.

**Disposition:** proof-only strengthening. C-II and Level I remain open.
